00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Italy has become Caffchiana. This is the exact question from which today's investigation starts.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
There is a truly fascinating institutional indigm at the heart of modern democracies.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,000
How and above all because they risk sinking into irreparable legislative chaos.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Through the lens of a fundamental economic study on the overproduction of law and political instability

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,000
and deserves a very clear picture.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,000
This is a real investigation into the hidden mechanisms that link politicians' decisions

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:30,000
efficiency of the state machine.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
The central question is precisely this, how the involution of institutions takes place.

9
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,000
There are state walls that work with zvizeri comeologists and others that look like traps with no way out.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Now, the real unknown is not just because these differences exist,

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
but how an originally virtuous system can deteriorate inexorably over time.

12
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Let's not talk about a trivial road accident,

13
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,000
but a structural deterioration that follows the economic rule and very precise incentives.

14
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Section 1, the mystery of the abhorrent craze.

15
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Therefore, to understand this deterioration it is necessary first to establish the two extremes of the institutional spectrum,

16
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
the absolute order and the caostotal.

17
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
On the one hand we have the ideal of the ineato by Max Weber,

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
that is, an abrupt craze that guarantees order and maximizes efficiency.

19
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
On the other, there is the nightmare described by Franz Kafka.

20
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,000
A ministry characterized by an absolutely disorienting complexity.

21
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
A perfect historical example is the Sburg Empire.

22
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,000
In the 19th century it was considered a model of Weberian efficiency,

23
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,000
but by the beginning of the twentieth century it had literally collapsed into a Caffchian state.

24
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,000
You might think that for the payment of a single Vienna tax

25
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
the involvement of 27 public officials had become necessary.

26
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
27, a huge leap.

27
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Section 2, the choice of the incompetent politician.

28
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
The reputational economic incentives of politicians.

29
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,000
The crucial point, in fact, is the combination of time available to a politician

30
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:01,000
and the speed of abrupt crap.

31
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
A politician, especially if not very competent,

32
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,000
wish to hopefully build a reputation

33
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
as a skilled reformer in view of the subsequent elections.

34
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
But if the political horizon is short and the abhorrent craze is slow,

35
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,000
creates an incentive for verse.

36
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Incompetent politicians start approving junk or low-quality

37
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
simply to appear active.

38
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
They know perfectly well that the slowness of the bureaucratic system

39
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,000
will prevent voters from assessing the real failure of these reforms

40
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,000
prior to the time of voting.

41
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Section 3, from Weber to Kafka.

42
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Turning to the visual economic evidence,

43
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:37,000
it is understood exactly where this institutional short-circumcision takes place.

44
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,000
On the horizontal side, we have the stock of reforms waiting to be implemented

45
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,000
on the vertical one, inefficiency.

46
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,000
The point marked with the letter V double represents the status

47
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Weberian, few pending laws and maximum deficiency,

48
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
where politicians propose useful reforms.

49
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
But be careful, because if the volume of laws accumulates beyond

50
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
a certain threshold, efficiency drops drastically, bringing the system

51
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
towards the kappa point, the Kafka balance flat.

52
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
In this abyss, the machine is so engulfed that incompetence

53
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,000
policy thrives without any control.

54
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
This brilliantly illustrates what happens when the system

55
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:13,000
suffers a shock.

56
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
The red dotted line represents a period of political instability,

57
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
an event that shortens the lives of governments.

58
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,000
This instability pushes politicians to generate a nomala wave

59
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:23,000
of new laws.

60
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
The sudden spike pushes the stock of pending reforms over the threshold

61
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,000
criticism, getting to the kappa point first.

62
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
And the worrying thing is that even when the initial shock wears off,

63
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
bureaucracy is now so overloaded that the system remains

64
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,000
trapped in his Kafka flat nightmare.

65
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Section 4.

66
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,000
The case of the two republics.

67
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,000
The theory finds a perfect match in historical reality.

68
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,000
The investigation moves to the scene of an epochal transition.

69
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Italy, after the fall of the Berlin Wall, in 1989.

70
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
The transition from the first to the second republic offers a real and

71
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,000
own natural laboratory to test the model.

72
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,000
The first republic was characterized by a strong stability

73
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
of the political agenda and by governments with long time horizons.

74
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
With the end of the Cold War, the system suffered an exogenous shock,

75
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
inaugurating a second republic marked by a very high

76
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
instability and weak and fragmented qualitions.

77
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
The parliamentarians' change of uniform has literally exploded.

78
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Changing party politicians increased from 1.8%

79
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
at 8.7%.

80
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,000
The political horizon in soma had drastically shortened.

81
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,000
In the face of this profound instability,

82
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
legislative activism has gone mad.

83
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
The production of new laws has increased dramatically.

84
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:38,000
During the first republic, Parliament approved on average

85
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,000
261,000 words of legislation every quarter.

86
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:46,000
In the second republic, this number jumped to 475,000 words.

87
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:52,000
This is an explosive 86% increase, a real bump on a regulatory AMI.

88
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
But beware, it's not just a matter of quantity.

89
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
The real problem is the collapse of quality.

90
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:01,000
The linguistic analysis of tens of thousands of laws

91
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
revealed that the new rules are written much, much worse.

92
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Sentences became 21% longer,

93
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
the use of gerund, which is a known enemy of legal clarity,

94
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,000
is even quadrupled.

95
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Laws have become chaotic, full of lengthy preambles

96
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,000
and continuous cross-references to other regulations.

97
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:24,000
All of this has created a deep and disorienting structural ambiguity.

98
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,000
And clearly this overdose of badly written laws

99
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
had a measurable impact.

100
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,000
International indices assessing Italian bureaucratic efficiency

101
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
precipitated rapidly.

102
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
At the same time, the gap has become tangible in society.

103
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,000
The use of the bureaucratic word and the headlines of the main newspapers

104
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:42,000
has tripled.

105
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
The system had officially entered its Caffchia phase,

106
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,000
where too many reforms had in fact paralyzed the implementation of any reform.

107
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Section 5. Comparison with Germany.

108
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Now, to scientifically validate this theory

109
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
you need a control group.

110
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:02,000
And Germany is the ideal comparison.

111
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Germany has faced the same identical global historical shocks,

112
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
including the fall of the Berlin Wall and European integration.

113
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:13,000
However, it has maintained a highly stable political system,

114
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,000
without undergoing thatshortening of political horizons that we've seen before.

115
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:18,000
And what was the result?

116
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
No explosion in legislative production.

117
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:24,000
The amount of legitimate deschi has remained constant over time

118
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,000
and the quality of the regulatory syntax has not deteriorated.

119
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
As a result, the efficiency of bureaucracy in Germany

120
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,000
has remained in interruption at the highest levels.

121
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:39,000
Political instability is in all respects the true and only spark of disaster.

122
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
This brings us to the long-term consequence,

123
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
perhaps the most tragic of them all.

124
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
Defined as Gresham's law of bureaucracy.

125
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
In a Veberian and efficient system,

126
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
politics among talented people,

127
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:55,000
precisely because reforms are implemented and merit is recognized.

128
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,000
But when the bureaucracy becomes Caffchian,

129
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,000
reforms are lost in thin air.

130
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:02,000
The chronic inability to implement real change,

131
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,000
inevitably discourages the most competent individuals

132
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,000
from interlearning the political career.

133
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,000
The system is then left to incompetent politicians,

134
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:14,000
ensuring that this nightmare continues to feed itself endlessly.

135
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:18,000
It remains a fundamental question about the dynamics of modern democracies.

136
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
It's really possible to reverse course,

137
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
once the Caffchiano equilibrium has been reached,

138
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,000
drastically reduce the laws,

139
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,000
simplify codes or even put legislative moratoriums,

140
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,000
would bring the normal structural benefits, without a doubt.

141
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:36,000
However, for politicians trapped in narrow time horizons,

142
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,000
promote a painful simplification

143
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
does not offer any immediate electoral return.

144
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,000
Escaping this labyrinth requires a very long term vision.

145
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
A feature that unfortunately political instability

146
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
inexorably tends to can...
